INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','easy','mcq','Which ligament suspends the ovary from the lateral pelvic wall and transmits the ovarian vessels?','["A. Infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament","B. Round ligament","C. Utero-ovarian ligament","D. Cardinal ligament"]','A','The infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament contains the ovarian artery, vein, and lymphatics and suspends the ovary from the lateral pelvic wall. The utero-ovarian ligament connects the ovary to the uterus, while the round ligament maintains uterine anteversion and the cardinal ligament supports the cervix.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','easy','mcq','The uterine artery is a branch of which vessel?','["A. External iliac artery","B. Internal iliac artery","C. Aorta","D. Ovarian artery"]','B','The uterine artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac (hypogastric) artery. It crosses the ureter (''water under the bridge'') at the level of the internal os and anastomoses with the ovarian artery. This anatomical relationship is clinically important during hysterectomy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','medium','mcq','The inner functional layer of the endometrium that is shed during menstruation is called the:','["A. Stratum basalis","B. Stratum compactum only","C. Stratum functionalis","D. Myometrium"]','C','The endometrium has two layers: the stratum functionalis (the inner, superficial layer) is shed during menstruation and responds to hormonal changes, while the stratum basalis (the deeper layer adjacent to myometrium) remains and regenerates the functionalis after each cycle.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','medium','mcq','Which segment of the fallopian tube is the most common site of fertilisation?','["A. Infundibulum","B. Interstitial portion","C. Isthmus","D. Ampulla"]','D','Fertilisation typically occurs in the ampulla of the fallopian tube, which is the widest and longest segment. The ovum is transported from the fimbriae to the ampulla where it meets capacitated spermatozoa. Transport of the fertilised egg to the uterus takes 3–4 days.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','medium','mcq','Which cell type within the ovarian follicle directly surrounds the oocyte and secretes oestrogen via aromatase?','["A. Granulosa cells","B. Theca interna cells","C. Theca externa cells","D. Interstitial cells"]','A','Granulosa cells directly surround the oocyte and form the cumulus oophorus. They express aromatase (CYP19A1) stimulated by FSH, converting androgens supplied by theca interna cells into oestradiol (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model). They also secrete inhibin B and anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','hard','mcq','During hysterectomy, the surgeon ligates the uterine artery 2 cm lateral to the cervix. Which structure is most at risk of inadvertent injury?','["A. External iliac vein","B. Ureter","C. Obturator nerve","D. Sigmoid colon"]','B','The ureter runs approximately 1–2 cm lateral to the cervix and passes beneath the uterine artery (''water under the bridge'') before entering the bladder. Ligation of the uterine artery risks inadvertent ureteric ligation or transection, a recognised complication of hysterectomy occurring in ~0.5% of cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','easy','mcq','Lymphatic drainage of the uterine body and fundus primarily goes to which lymph nodes?','["A. Superficial inguinal nodes","B. Deep inguinal nodes","C. Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","D. Obturator nodes"]','C','The body of the uterus and fundus drain primarily to the para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes along the ovarian vessels. The cervix drains to internal iliac, external iliac, and obturator nodes. Understanding lymphatic drainage is critical for staging gynaecological cancers.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','medium','mcq','The pouch of Douglas (rectouterine pouch) is bounded anteriorly by which structure?','["A. Sigmoid colon","B. Rectum","C. Posterior wall of the bladder","D. Posterior wall of the uterus and posterior vaginal fornix"]','D','The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) is bounded anteriorly by the posterior wall of the uterus and the posterior vaginal fornix, and posteriorly by the rectum. It is the most dependent peritoneal recess in the erect female and a common site for fluid/blood collection, detectable by transvaginal ultrasound or culdocentesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','hard','mcq','The spiral arteries of the endometrium are derived from which source vessel?','["A. Radial arteries of the myometrium","B. Arcuate arteries directly","C. Uterine artery directly","D. Ovarian artery"]','A','The uterine artery branches into arcuate arteries within the outer myometrium, which give rise to radial arteries penetrating toward the endometrium. Radial arteries then branch into straight (basal) arteries supplying the stratum basalis and spiral arteries supplying the stratum functionalis. Spiral arteries are uniquely sensitive to progesterone withdrawal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Reproductive Anatomy','hard','mcq','Which nerve provides the primary sensory and motor innervation to the external genitalia and perineum, and is blocked for obstetric analgesia?','["A. Ilioinguinal nerve","B. Pudendal nerve","C. Genitofemoral nerve","D. Posterior femoral cutaneous nerve"]','B','The pudendal nerve (S2–S4) provides the primary sensory and motor innervation to the external genitalia, perineum, and external urethral/anal sphincters. It passes through Alcock''s canal and can be blocked via a pudendal nerve block for obstetric procedures. Injury during forceps delivery can cause perineal anaesthesia and sphincter dysfunction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','easy','mcq','In a typical 28-day menstrual cycle, ovulation occurs on approximately which day?','["A. Day 7","B. Day 21","C. Day 14","D. Day 28"]','C','Ovulation typically occurs on day 14 of a 28-day cycle, approximately 36–48 hours after the LH surge. The follicular phase (days 1–14) is variable in length, whereas the luteal phase is relatively fixed at 14 days. Cycle length variation therefore affects the pre-ovulatory period.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','easy','mcq','What hormone surge triggers ovulation approximately 36–48 hours later?','["A. FSH surge","B. Progesterone surge","C. Oestrogen surge","D. LH surge"]','D','The mid-cycle LH surge (triggered by positive feedback from rising oestradiol >200 pg/mL for >48 hours) triggers ovulation approximately 36–48 hours later. LH activates proteolytic enzymes in the follicle wall, stimulates prostaglandin production, and causes cumulus expansion, allowing follicular rupture and oocyte release.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','mcq','After ovulation, the corpus luteum normally functions for approximately how long before involuting?','["A. 12–14 days","B. 9–11 days","C. 5–7 days","D. 20–25 days"]','A','The corpus luteum has a lifespan of approximately 12–14 days in the absence of pregnancy. If fertilisation occurs, hCG from the trophoblast maintains the corpus luteum. Without hCG, luteolysis occurs around day 26–28, progesterone falls, and menstruation follows 1–2 days later.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','mcq','During the follicular phase, which hormone drives proliferation of granulosa cells and causes endometrial proliferation?','["A. Progesterone","B. Oestradiol","C. LH","D. FSH alone"]','B','Oestradiol (17β-oestradiol) secreted by granulosa cells is the primary driver of endometrial proliferation during the follicular phase. FSH stimulates granulosa cell proliferation and aromatase activity, but it is the resulting oestradiol that acts on the endometrium. Progesterone is predominantly a luteal-phase hormone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','mcq','A basal body temperature (BBT) chart shows a rise of 0.3–0.5°C persisting for 12 days. This is caused by the thermogenic effect of:','["A. Oestrogen","B. FSH","C. Progesterone","D. LH"]','C','Progesterone produced by the corpus luteum after ovulation has a thermogenic effect, raising basal body temperature by 0.3–0.5°C. This temperature rise confirms that ovulation has occurred and persists throughout the luteal phase, returning to baseline at menstruation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','hard','mcq','During the LH surge, which mechanism shifts granulosa cell steroid output from oestrogen to progesterone?','["A. Downregulation of aromatase by inhibin","B. Progesterone positive feedback on granulosa cells","C. FSH withdrawal decreasing androgen substrate","D. LH-induced luteinisation upregulating 3β-HSD and reducing aromatase activity"]','D','The LH surge triggers luteinisation of granulosa cells: expression of 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (3β-HSD) increases and aromatase activity decreases. This shifts steroidogenesis from oestrogen production to progesterone production. Progesterone then contributes to positive feedback on the LH surge and prepares the endometrium for implantation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','easy','mcq','What change in cervical mucus is characteristic of the peri-ovulatory period?','["A. Thin, watery, acellular, with maximum spinnbarkeit and ferning pattern","B. Thick, scanty, and cellular","C. Thick with high progesterone content","D. Absent during ovulation"]','A','At ovulation, rising oestrogen causes cervical mucus to become thin, watery, acellular, and profuse with maximum spinnbarkeit (stretchiness up to 10 cm). When dried on a slide it shows a ferning (arborisation) pattern. After ovulation, progesterone makes the mucus thick, scant, and cellular, blocking sperm penetration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','medium','mcq','At the end of a cycle without pregnancy, which hormonal change initiates recruitment of a new follicle cohort?','["A. Rising progesterone → LH suppression → menstruation","B. Falling oestrogen and progesterone removing negative feedback → FSH rise","C. hCG maintaining corpus luteum → continued progesterone","D. Rising inhibin B → FSH suppression"]','B','When the corpus luteum involutes without pregnancy, oestrogen and progesterone fall sharply, removing negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary. GnRH and then FSH rise, recruiting a new cohort of antral follicles and initiating the next follicular phase.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','hard','mcq','A woman presents with secondary amenorrhoea and low FSH. Prolactin is elevated. How does hyperprolactinaemia cause anovulation?','["A. Direct inhibition of LH receptors on granulosa cells","B. Stimulation of adrenal androgen production","C. Suppression of pulsatile GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus","D. Direct luteolysis of the corpus luteum"]','C','Hyperprolactinaemia suppresses pulsatile GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus (via increased hypothalamic dopamine tone and endogenous opioids). Reduced GnRH pulsatility leads to diminished FSH and LH release, resulting in anovulation and amenorrhoea. Dopamine agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline) restore ovulation by reducing prolactin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Menstrual Cycle & Ovulation','hard','mcq','The dominant follicle suppresses co-follicle growth primarily by which mechanism?','["A. High androgen secretion causing atresia of smaller follicles","B. LH receptor upregulation causing direct suppression of adjacent follicles","C. Direct paracrine inhibition via gap junctions between follicles","D. Secretion of oestrogen and inhibin B reducing FSH, which smaller follicles depend on"]','D','The dominant follicle produces high amounts of oestradiol and inhibin B, suppressing pituitary FSH secretion. Smaller, less FSH-sensitive follicles are deprived of FSH support and undergo atresia. The dominant follicle is less FSH-dependent because it has upregulated FSH receptors and has acquired LH receptors, allowing it to continue growing despite falling FSH.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','easy','mcq','Oestrogen synthesis requires cooperation between two ovarian cell types. Which cells provide androgen precursors and which cells convert them to oestrogen?','["A. Theca interna (androgens) → granulosa cells (aromatase)","B. Granulosa cells (androgens) → theca interna (aromatase)","C. Theca externa (androgens) → granulosa cells (aromatase)","D. Interstitial cells (androgens) → theca interna (aromatase)"]','A','The two-cell, two-gonadotropin theory: LH stimulates theca interna cells to produce androgens (androstenedione, testosterone) from cholesterol. FSH stimulates granulosa cells to express aromatase (CYP19A1) which converts these androgens to oestradiol. Neither cell type alone can produce significant oestrogen.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','easy','mcq','Which is the most potent naturally occurring oestrogen?','["A. Oestriol (E3)","B. Oestradiol (E2)","C. Oestrone (E1)","D. Oestetrol (E4)"]','B','Oestradiol (17β-oestradiol, E2) is the most potent naturally occurring oestrogen with the highest affinity for oestrogen receptors ERα and ERβ. Oestrone (E1) is less potent and predominates after menopause. Oestriol (E3) is the weakest and is produced in large quantities by the placenta during pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','mcq','Progesterone exerts which effect on the endometrium during the secretory phase?','["A. Stimulates stromal proliferation and glandular straightening","B. Increases spiral artery number without altering gland secretion","C. Converts proliferative endometrium to secretory pattern with coiled glands and glycogen-rich stroma","D. Inhibits endometrial growth without producing secretory changes"]','C','Progesterone acts on the oestrogen-primed endometrium to convert it to the secretory phase: glands become coiled and filled with glycogen-rich secretions, stroma becomes oedematous and decidualised, and spiral arteries lengthen. These changes create a receptive environment for blastocyst implantation (window of implantation, days 20–24).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','mcq','Sustained oestradiol levels >200 pg/mL for >48 hours switch oestrogen''s feedback effect on the pituitary from negative to positive, causing:','["A. Progesterone surge","B. FSH-only surge","C. Inhibin B surge","D. Mid-cycle LH surge triggering ovulation"]','D','High, sustained oestradiol concentrations >200 pg/mL for >48–50 hours switch the pituitary and hypothalamic response from negative to positive feedback, resulting in the mid-cycle LH surge (with a smaller FSH surge). This positive feedback mechanism is a unique feature of female reproductive endocrinology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','mcq','Oestrogen''s effect on bone metabolism includes all of the following EXCEPT:','["A. Direct inhibition of PTH synthesis from the parathyroid glands","B. Promotion of osteoblast differentiation","C. Stimulation of calcitonin release","D. Inhibition of osteoclast activity via OPG/RANK-L pathway"]','A','Oestrogen inhibits osteoclast activity (by reducing RANK-L and increasing OPG), promotes osteoblast activity, and stimulates calcitonin release. Oestrogen does not directly inhibit PTH synthesis; rather, it reduces bone sensitivity to PTH. Oestrogen deficiency at menopause accelerates bone resorption, increasing osteoporosis risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','hard','mcq','Which enzyme converts progesterone to 17α-hydroxyprogesterone, a key branch-point intermediate in cortisol and sex hormone synthesis?','["A. 21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2)","B. 17α-hydroxylase (CYP17A1)","C. 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","D. 11β-hydroxylase (CYP11B1)"]','B','CYP17A1 (17α-hydroxylase) converts progesterone to 17α-hydroxyprogesterone and pregnenolone to 17α-hydroxypregnenolone. This enzyme is essential for cortisol and sex steroid synthesis. Deficiency of CYP17A1 blocks cortisol and androgen/oestrogen synthesis, causing 46,XY DSD. 21-hydroxylase deficiency (most common CAH) affects mineralocorticoid/cortisol but shunts toward androgens.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','easy','mcq','Which protein is the primary carrier of oestrogen and testosterone in the circulation?','["A. Albumin","B. Corticosteroid-binding globulin (CBG)","C. Sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG)","D. Transthyretin"]','C','Sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), produced by the liver, binds oestradiol and testosterone with high affinity. Approximately 40% of oestradiol is bound to SHBG and 60% to albumin (lower affinity). Only free (unbound) hormone is biologically active. Oestrogen increases SHBG production; androgens and insulin decrease it.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','hard','mcq','A 22-year-old with 46,XX karyotype presents with primary amenorrhoea and anosmia. Hormones show low FSH, low LH, low oestrogen. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Turner syndrome — low oestrogen, high FSH/LH","B. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia — high androgens","C. Complete androgen insensitivity — requires XY karyotype","D. Kallmann syndrome — hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism with anosmia"]','D','Kallmann syndrome is caused by failure of GnRH neuron migration (KAL1/ANOS1, FGFR1 mutations), resulting in hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism with anosmia (absent olfactory bulbs). Low GnRH → low FSH and LH → low oestrogen. This contrasts with Turner syndrome (hypergonadotrophic: high FSH/LH). 46,XX karyotype excludes androgen insensitivity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','medium','mcq','Which cardiovascular effect does progesterone produce?','["A. Vasodilation by upregulating nitric oxide synthase","B. Sodium and water retention","C. Vasoconstriction and increased peripheral resistance","D. Reduction in resting heart rate"]','A','Progesterone promotes vasodilation partly through upregulation of nitric oxide synthase. This contributes to the vasodilation and reduced systemic vascular resistance seen in the luteal phase and early pregnancy, accounting for the slight fall in blood pressure in the first trimester despite increased plasma volume.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Female Sex Hormones','hard','mcq','Tamoxifen is a SERM that is an oestrogen antagonist in breast tissue but an agonist in the uterus. This tissue specificity is because:','["A. Breast cells lack ERα receptors whereas uterine cells express only ERβ","B. Coregulator protein profiles differ — uterine coactivators convert the tamoxifen-ER complex into a transcriptional activator","C. Tamoxifen undergoes hepatic conversion to a uterine-specific metabolite","D. Uterine progesterone receptors cross-activate oestrogen target genes"]','B','The agonist/antagonist activity of SERMs is tissue-specific and depends on the coactivator and corepressor proteins expressed in each cell type. In breast cells, the tamoxifen-ER complex recruits corepressors causing gene suppression. In uterine cells, coactivators are recruited causing gene activation and endometrial proliferation — explaining the increased risk of endometrial cancer with tamoxifen use.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','easy','mcq','Fertilisation normally occurs in which part of the fallopian tube?','["A. Fimbriated end (infundibulum)","B. Isthmus","C. Ampulla","D. Interstitial portion"]','C','Fertilisation typically occurs in the ampulla of the fallopian tube. The ovum is transported from the fimbriae to the ampulla where it meets spermatozoa. Transport to the uterus takes 3–4 days during which the zygote undergoes cleavage divisions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','easy','mcq','Sperm capacitation involves which key membrane change?','["A. Sperm develop a flagellum de novo","B. Sperm nucleus undergoing meiosis II","C. Loss of mitochondria to allow acrosome exocytosis","D. Removal of cholesterol from the sperm plasma membrane, enabling the acrosome reaction"]','D','Capacitation occurs in the female reproductive tract (over 5–6 hours) and involves removal of cholesterol from the sperm plasma membrane, enabling hyperactivated motility and the acrosome reaction upon zona pellucida contact. It also involves protein tyrosine phosphorylation and cAMP increases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','mcq','The zona pellucida glycoprotein ZP3 triggers the acrosome reaction in capacitated sperm. What is released?','["A. Hyaluronidase and acrosin to penetrate the zona pellucida","B. Sperm nuclear decondensation enzymes","C. Calcium ions to fuse sperm with corona radiata cells","D. FSH-like molecules to resume oocyte meiosis I"]','A','The acrosome reaction is triggered by ZP3 binding to sperm receptors, causing calcium influx and exocytosis of acrosomal contents. Released enzymes include hyaluronidase (digests the corona radiata) and acrosin (a serine protease that penetrates the zona pellucida), allowing sperm to reach the oocyte plasma membrane.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','mcq','At the time of ovulation, the oocyte is arrested at which stage of meiosis?','["A. Prophase I","B. Metaphase II","C. Metaphase I","D. Telophase II"]','B','At ovulation, the oocyte is arrested at metaphase II of meiosis. Meiosis I was completed just before ovulation (expelling the first polar body). Meiosis II is completed only after fertilisation by sperm when calcium waves activate the oocyte, allowing extrusion of the second polar body.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','mcq','Implantation of the blastocyst normally occurs at which stage and timing after fertilisation?','["A. Morula stage, day 3","B. Two-cell stage, day 1","C. Blastocyst stage, days 6–10","D. Gastrula stage, day 14"]','C','Implantation occurs at the blastocyst stage, typically days 6–10 after fertilisation (days 20–24 of the menstrual cycle — the window of implantation). The blastocyst hatches from the zona pellucida and the trophoblast invades the prepared endometrium, most commonly on the posterior wall of the uterine fundus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','hard','mcq','After sperm-oocyte fusion, what is the permanent block to polyspermy?','["A. FSH surge blocking sperm motility","B. Cervical mucus barrier preventing additional sperm entry","C. Release of inhibin from the fertilised oocyte","D. Zona reaction — cortical granule exocytosis hardens the zona pellucida"]','D','Polyspermy is prevented by two mechanisms: (1) the fast block — immediate membrane depolarisation (seconds); (2) the zona reaction (permanent block) — cortical granules exocytose and modify ZP2 and ZP3 within 1–2 minutes, hardening the zona pellucida to prevent additional sperm binding. The zona reaction is the definitive block.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','easy','mcq','The inner cell mass (embryoblast) of the blastocyst gives rise to:','["A. The fetus and some extra-embryonic membranes (amnion, yolk sac)","B. Placenta and chorion only","C. Only the amnion","D. Only the syncytiotrophoblast"]','A','The inner cell mass (embryoblast) gives rise to the fetus (all three germ layers), the amnion, the primary yolk sac, and the extraembryonic mesoderm. The trophoblast (outer cell layer) gives rise to the placenta, chorion, and syncytiotrophoblast.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','medium','mcq','hCG first becomes detectable in maternal blood approximately how many days after fertilisation?','["A. 2–3 days","B. 6–8 days","C. 14–16 days","D. 28 days"]','B','hCG is secreted by the syncytiotrophoblast from approximately day 6–8 after fertilisation (around the time of implantation). It becomes detectable in maternal serum by days 8–10, allowing very early pregnancy detection. hCG doubles approximately every 48–72 hours in early pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','hard','mcq','Endometrial biopsy in a patient with recurrent implantation failure shows reduced αvβ3 integrins and absent pinopodes. These are markers of failure of which process?','["A. Endometrial hyperplasia","B. Endometrial atrophy from oestrogen deficiency","C. The window of implantation — peak endometrial receptivity (days 20–24)","D. Chronic endometritis"]','C','The window of implantation (approximately days 20–24 of a 28-day cycle) is characterised by maximal endometrial receptivity, marked by upregulation of αvβ3 integrins, pinopodes (apical membrane projections), and LIF (leukaemia inhibitory factor). Reduced expression indicates impaired receptivity and can be assessed by the endometrial receptivity array (ERA).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Fertilisation & Early Embryology','hard','mcq','Which trophoblast subtype remodels spiral arteries to ensure adequate uteroplacental blood flow?','["A. Syncytiotrophoblast","B. Villous cytotrophoblast","C. Trophoblast giant cells","D. Extravillous trophoblast (interstitial and endovascular)"]','D','Extravillous trophoblast (EVT) cells invade into the decidua/myometrium (interstitial EVT) and into spiral artery lumens (endovascular EVT). Endovascular EVT replaces the endothelium and destroys the muscular wall, converting narrow high-resistance spiral arteries into wide low-resistance conduits. Failure of this remodelling is implicated in pre-eclampsia and fetal growth restriction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','easy','mcq','The early placental barrier (placental membrane) consists of which layers that oxygen must cross?','["A. Syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast, connective tissue, fetal endothelium","B. Maternal endothelium only","C. Maternal decidua, syncytiotrophoblast, fetal endothelium","D. Amnion, chorion, syncytiotrophoblast, fetal endothelium"]','A','The early placental barrier consists of 4 layers: syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast, connective tissue (mesenchyme), and fetal endothelium. By the third trimester, cytotrophoblast cells thin and become discontinuous, reducing the barrier to essentially syncytiotrophoblast and fetal endothelium (vasculosyncytial membrane), facilitating gas exchange.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','easy','mcq','hCG produced by the placenta peaks at which week of gestation?','["A. 4 weeks","B. 8–10 weeks","C. 20 weeks","D. 38–40 weeks"]','B','hCG is produced by the syncytiotrophoblast and peaks at 8–10 weeks, then declines to a plateau by 16–20 weeks. The peak hCG coincides with maximum corpus luteum stimulation (luteo-placental shift occurs at ~10 weeks). Very high hCG is seen in molar pregnancy and trisomy 21.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','mcq','Oxygen transfer across the placenta primarily occurs by which mechanism?','["A. Active transport requiring ATP","B. Facilitated diffusion via specialised oxygen carriers","C. Simple diffusion down a partial pressure gradient","D. Pinocytosis"]','C','Oxygen crosses the placental membrane by simple diffusion down its partial pressure gradient. Maternal PO2 in the intervillous space (~50 mmHg) exceeds fetal umbilical vein PO2 (~30 mmHg). Fetal haemoglobin (HbF) has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, further facilitating oxygen transfer.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','mcq','Human placental lactogen (hPL) has which primary metabolic effect on the mother?','["A. Insulin sensitisation to promote glucose uptake","B. Inhibition of lipolysis to conserve maternal fat stores","C. Stimulation of maternal glucagon secretion","D. Diabetogenic effect — insulin resistance increasing glucose availability for the fetus"]','D','hPL (human chorionic somatomammotropin) has anti-insulin (diabetogenic) effects, causing maternal insulin resistance. This increases maternal blood glucose and free fatty acids, providing more nutrients for the fetus. hPL levels rise progressively through pregnancy correlating with placental mass and is a major contributor to gestational diabetes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','mcq','Glucose transport across the placenta to the fetus is mediated by which transporter?','["A. GLUT1 facilitated diffusion transporters on syncytiotrophoblast","B. SGLT1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter) — active transport","C. Simple diffusion along a concentration gradient","D. GLUT4 insulin-responsive transporters"]','A','Glucose crosses the placenta via facilitated diffusion using GLUT1 transporters expressed on both the maternal-facing (microvillous) and fetal-facing (basal) membranes of the syncytiotrophoblast. Fetal blood glucose is approximately 20–30 mg/dL lower than maternal, maintaining the gradient for continuous fetal supply.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','hard','mcq','The placenta lacks which enzyme, making it unable to convert progesterone to oestrogen precursors without fetal adrenal input?','["A. Aromatase (CYP19A1)","B. 17α-hydroxylase (CYP17A1)","C. 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","D. Cholesterol desmolase (CYP11A1)"]','B','The syncytiotrophoblast lacks 17α-hydroxylase (CYP17A1), meaning it cannot convert progesterone to 17α-hydroxyprogesterone (the gateway to sex steroid synthesis). The placenta therefore relies on fetal adrenal DHEA-S as androgen precursor for oestrogen synthesis — the basis of the fetoplacental unit concept.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','easy','mcq','The double Bohr effect facilitates oxygen transfer at the placenta. Which statement correctly describes this?','["A. Rising CO2 in maternal blood causes HbF to release oxygen","B. Rising pH in fetal blood shifts fetal ODC right, increasing O2 release to tissues","C. CO2 diffusing from fetal to maternal blood lowers maternal pH, shifting maternal ODC right; fetal pH rising shifts fetal ODC left — both augment O2 transfer","D. Fetal haemoglobin has higher CO2 affinity than HbA"]','C','The double Bohr effect: CO2 diffuses from fetal to maternal blood. This raises maternal pCO2 and lowers maternal pH, shifting the maternal ODC rightward (releasing more O2 from HbA). Simultaneously, as CO2 leaves fetal blood, fetal pH rises, shifting the fetal ODC leftward (increasing HbF affinity for O2). Both effects augment O2 transfer from mother to fetus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','hard','mcq','Placental biopsy in a patient with severe pre-eclampsia at 32 weeks would most likely show:','["A. Excessive trophoblast invasion of the myometrium","B. Hydropic villi with absent vasculature","C. Increased VEGF expression in placental villi","D. Incomplete remodelling of spiral arteries with persistent muscular walls"]','D','Pre-eclampsia is associated with deficient trophoblast invasion and incomplete spiral artery remodelling. Spiral arteries retain their muscular walls and remain narrow high-resistance vessels, reducing uteroplacental perfusion. Placental hypoxia triggers release of anti-angiogenic factors (sFlt-1, sEng) that damage maternal endothelium, producing hypertension and proteinuria.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','medium','mcq','Iron transport across the placenta from mother to fetus uses which mechanism?','["A. Receptor-mediated endocytosis: transferrin-bound iron binds TfR1 on syncytiotrophoblast","B. Simple diffusion as free ionic iron","C. Active transport via ferroportin on the maternal surface only","D. Facilitated diffusion via DMT1 on maternal surface only"]','A','Iron transport is active and receptor-mediated. Maternal transferrin-bound iron binds transferrin receptor 1 (TfR1) on the microvillous membrane of the syncytiotrophoblast. The complex is endocytosed, iron is released in endosomes, exported across the basal membrane via ferroportin, and loaded onto fetal transferrin. This process is upregulated in maternal iron deficiency.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Placental Physiology','hard','mcq','The placenta produces oestriol (E3) using which fetal precursor?','["A. Cholesterol from the fetal liver","B. 16α-hydroxy-DHEA-S from the fetal adrenal gland and liver","C. Progesterone from the corpus luteum","D. Cortisol from the fetal adrenal cortex"]','B','The fetoplacental unit: fetal adrenal cortex secretes large amounts of DHEA-S, which is hydroxylated at position 16 in the fetal liver to form 16α-hydroxy-DHEA-S. This travels to the placenta and is converted via aromatase to oestriol (E3). Low unconjugated oestriol in the triple/quad screen suggests fetal compromise (oestriol is a marker of fetoplacental function).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','easy','mcq','By what percentage does plasma volume increase during normal pregnancy?','["A. 10–15%","B. 20–30%","C. 40–50%","D. 70–80%"]','C','Plasma volume increases by approximately 40–50% during pregnancy (from ~2600 mL to ~3800 mL), beginning at ~6 weeks and peaking at 28–34 weeks. Red cell mass increases by only ~20–30%, creating dilutional (physiological) anaemia of pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','easy','mcq','By term, cardiac output increases by approximately what percentage above pre-pregnancy values?','["A. 10%","B. 25–30%","C. 70–80%","D. 40–50%"]','D','Cardiac output increases by 40–50% during pregnancy, beginning in the first trimester and peaking at 28–32 weeks. This is achieved by increases in both stroke volume (~25–30%) and heart rate (~15–20 bpm). Systemic vascular resistance decreases due to progesterone- and oestrogen-mediated vasodilation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','mcq','A woman at 20 weeks gestation develops light-headedness when lying supine. The mechanism is:','["A. Aortocaval compression by the gravid uterus reducing venous return and cardiac output","B. Anaemia causing reduced O2 delivery","C. Hypoglycaemia from fetal glucose consumption","D. Progesterone-induced orthostatic hypotension"]','A','Supine hypotension syndrome of pregnancy: the gravid uterus compresses the inferior vena cava when the mother lies supine, reducing venous return and cardiac output. Relief by left lateral positioning is immediate. This is why pregnant women beyond 20 weeks are positioned in left lateral tilt during resuscitation or surgery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','mcq','Which respiratory change does NOT occur in normal pregnancy?','["A. Increased tidal volume (~40%)","B. Significant increase in respiratory rate","C. Decreased functional residual capacity (FRC) (~20%)","D. Mild compensated respiratory alkalosis (PaCO2 ~30 mmHg)"]','B','In normal pregnancy: tidal volume increases ~40%, FRC decreases ~20% (diaphragm elevation), minute ventilation increases ~40%, and mild chronic respiratory alkalosis occurs. Respiratory rate does not significantly increase. The dyspnoea of pregnancy is due to increased tidal volume and progesterone-driven increased CO2 sensitivity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','mcq','GFR increases by approximately what percentage by the end of the first trimester of pregnancy?','["A. 10–15%","B. 25%","C. 40–65%","D. 80–100%"]','C','GFR increases by 40–65% during pregnancy, beginning as early as 4–6 weeks, due to increased renal plasma flow and hyperfiltration driven by relaxin, progesterone, and nitric oxide. This causes lower serum creatinine (normal <0.8 mg/dL in pregnancy vs <1.0 mg/dL non-pregnant), and glycosuria at normal blood glucose.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','hard','mcq','A pregnant woman at 30 weeks has serum sodium 134 mEq/L and serum osmolality 270 mOsm/kg and is asymptomatic. What is the most likely explanation?','["A. SIADH requiring fluid restriction","B. Adrenal insufficiency","C. Pre-eclampsia-related hyponatraemia","D. Physiological dilutional hyponatraemia — osmotic set-point is reset downward in pregnancy"]','D','During pregnancy, the osmotic threshold for ADH release and thirst is reset downward by approximately 10 mOsm/kg (to ~270–275 mOsm/kg), driven by hCG and relaxin. This creates physiological dilutional hyponatraemia (serum Na typically 132–136 mEq/L) that is normal and asymptomatic. No treatment is required.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','easy','mcq','Which coagulation change in normal pregnancy increases the risk of venous thromboembolism?','["A. Increased fibrinogen and procoagulant factors with decreased protein S","B. Decreased fibrinogen and reduced factors V, VIII","C. Thrombocytopenia with elevated PT/APTT","D. Increased protein C and antithrombin III"]','A','Pregnancy creates a hypercoagulable state: fibrinogen increases 50%, factors VII, VIII, X, XII, and vWF increase, while anticoagulant protein S decreases and acquired APC resistance develops. Combined with venous stasis and endothelial changes, VTE risk increases 5-fold in pregnancy and 20-fold postpartum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','hard','mcq','Which placental hormone is the PRIMARY driver of progressive insulin resistance in mid-to-late pregnancy?','["A. Progesterone","B. Human placental lactogen (hPL)","C. Oestradiol","D. Cortisol"]','B','hPL (human placental lactogen/chorionic somatomammotropin) is the primary driver of progressive insulin resistance in mid-to-late pregnancy. hPL levels rise in proportion to placental mass and peak at term. hPL impairs insulin signalling and promotes lipolysis, releasing FFAs as alternative maternal fuel and sparing glucose for the fetus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','medium','mcq','Which statement about relaxin in pregnancy is correct?','["A. Stimulates uterine contractions; reduces joint laxity","B. Inhibits progesterone production; stimulates lactation","C. Increases GFR and renal plasma flow; promotes cervical ripening; increases joint laxity by remodelling collagen","D. Causes vasoconstriction to support placental perfusion"]','C','Relaxin is produced by the corpus luteum (first trimester) and then the placenta. Its roles include: renal vasodilation (increases GFR and RPF), cervical ripening/softening, pubic symphysis and pelvic ligament relaxation, and uterine quiescence. Excessive joint laxity from relaxin contributes to pelvic girdle pain in pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Pregnancy Physiology','hard','mcq','A woman at 12 weeks pregnancy has serum creatinine of 1.2 mg/dL. How should this be interpreted?','["A. Normal for pregnancy","B. Indicates pre-eclampsia","C. Consistent with physiological haemodilution","D. Likely represents significant renal impairment given the expected 40–65% increase in GFR"]','D','Because GFR increases by 40–65% in pregnancy, serum creatinine normally falls to <0.8 mg/dL. A serum creatinine of 1.2 mg/dL — which would be normal outside pregnancy — represents a doubling of the expected creatinine and indicates significant renal impairment in a pregnant woman. Pre-eclampsia is unlikely at 12 weeks but underlying renal disease should be investigated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','easy','mcq','Progesterone maintains uterine quiescence during pregnancy primarily by:','["A. Reducing gap junction formation and suppressing oxytocin receptor expression","B. Stimulating myometrial contractions from early pregnancy","C. Sensitising the uterus to oxytocin from 20 weeks","D. Promoting cervical ripening throughout pregnancy"]','A','Progesterone (the hormone of pregnancy) maintains uterine quiescence by reducing myometrial gap junction formation (preventing synchronised contractions), suppressing oxytocin receptor expression, and stabilising lysosomes to prevent prostaglandin release. Functional progesterone withdrawal at term triggers parturition.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','easy','mcq','Which hormone administered intravenously causes sustained uterine contractions and is used to induce or augment labour?','["A. Prolactin","B. Oxytocin","C. Progesterone","D. hCG"]','B','Oxytocin, produced by the hypothalamus (paraventricular nucleus) and released by the posterior pituitary, binds myometrial oxytocin receptors (which increase 200-fold at term) and stimulates uterine contractions. Synthetic oxytocin (Syntocinon) is used to induce or augment labour. Oxytocin receptors are upregulated by oestrogen and downregulated by progesterone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','mcq','Cervical ripening before labour involves which biochemical process?','["A. Collagen synthesis and cross-linking causing stiffness","B. Progesterone-mediated decrease in collagenase activity","C. Collagenase-mediated dissolution of collagen cross-links with increased hydration and prostaglandin E2","D. Myosin light chain kinase activation in cervical smooth muscle"]','C','Cervical ripening involves: increased collagenase (MMP) activity dissolving collagen cross-links, increased hyaluronic acid (increasing water content), infiltration of neutrophils releasing MMPs, and prostaglandin E2 stimulating these changes. The cervix softens, effaces, and dilates. Cervical ripening agents (PGE2 gels, mechanical Foley catheter) mimic this process.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','mcq','The Ferguson reflex during labour describes which positive feedback mechanism?','["A. Fetal head descent triggering the maternal bearing-down reflex","B. PGE2 release inhibiting uterine contractions","C. Progesterone withdrawal triggering oxytocin synthesis","D. Cervical stretching → neural signals to hypothalamus → oxytocin release → more contractions → more dilation"]','D','The Ferguson reflex is a positive feedback loop: mechanical stretching of the cervix sends afferent neural signals via the pelvic nerve to the hypothalamus, stimulating oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary. More oxytocin → stronger contractions → more cervical dilation → more oxytocin. This continues until delivery removes the stimulus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','easy','mcq','Which stage of labour is defined as the period from full cervical dilation (10 cm) to delivery of the baby?','["A. Second stage","B. First stage","C. Third stage","D. Fourth stage"]','A','Stages of labour: First = onset of contractions to full cervical dilation. Second = full dilation to delivery of the baby. Third = delivery of the baby to delivery of the placenta. Fourth = first 2 hours postpartum (monitoring for haemorrhage).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','hard','mcq','Rising fetal cortisol at term contributes to initiating labour in humans primarily by:','["A. Crossing to the maternal circulation and directly stimulating contractions","B. Stimulating placental CYP17A1, shifting steroidogenesis from progesterone to oestrogen, increasing the oestrogen:progesterone ratio","C. Suppressing hCG production and reducing corpus luteum progesterone","D. Directly stimulating placental prostaglandin synthesis"]','B','In humans, rising fetal cortisol increases placental oestrogen synthesis by upregulating CYP17A1, diverting progesterone to oestrogen production and increasing the oestrogen:progesterone ratio. Rising oestrogen upregulates myometrial oxytocin receptors, gap junctions, and prostaglandin production, preparing the uterus for labour.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','mcq','Prostaglandins PGE2 and PGF2α promote uterine contractions and cervical ripening via which enzyme, inhibited by indomethacin (used as a tocolytic)?','["A. Phospholipase A2","B. Lipoxygenase","C. Cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2)","D. Thromboxane synthase"]','C','Arachidonic acid is converted to prostaglandins by cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, particularly COX-2 (upregulated in amnion and decidua at term). PGE2 and PGF2α promote uterine contractions and cervical ripening. Indomethacin (non-selective COX inhibitor) is used as a tocolytic to delay preterm labour, though fetal complications (premature ductus arteriosus closure) limit its use beyond 32 weeks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','hard','mcq','After placental delivery, the primary mechanism preventing haemorrhage is:','["A. Prostaglandin-mediated vasoconstriction of spiral arteries","B. Factor XIII cross-linking the placental bed","C. Fibrin deposition at the placental bed triggered by oxytocin","D. Uterine contraction compressing intramyometrial blood vessels — the ''living ligatures''"]','D','After placental delivery, uterine muscle fibres contract and retract, compressing the intramyometrial blood vessels (''living ligatures''). Failure of uterine contraction (uterine atony) is the most common cause of postpartum haemorrhage (~80% of cases) and is treated with uterotonic agents (oxytocin, ergometrine, carboprost).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','medium','mcq','A primigravida has weak, irregular uterine contractions with no progress in labour. Which agent augments labour by acting on Gq-coupled myometrial receptors?','["A. Intravenous oxytocin","B. Nifedipine (calcium channel blocker)","C. Indomethacin","D. Magnesium sulphate"]','A','IV oxytocin augments labour by binding Gq-coupled oxytocin receptors in myometrial cells, increasing intracellular calcium and activating myosin light chain kinase, producing coordinated contractions. Nifedipine, indomethacin, and magnesium sulphate are tocolytics (suppress contractions).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Parturition','hard','mcq','Which statement about placental CRH and parturition is INCORRECT?','["A. Placental CRH stimulates fetal adrenal cortisol/DHEAS production","B. Placental CRH continuously inhibits uterine contractions throughout pregnancy","C. Placental CRH promotes prostaglandin synthesis in the amnion","D. Placental CRH increases myometrial oxytocin receptor expression near term"]','B','Placental CRH stimulates fetal adrenal cortisol/DHEAS, promotes prostaglandin synthesis, and increases myometrial oxytocin receptor expression near term. Paradoxically, CRH also stimulates myometrial relaxation via cAMP during most of pregnancy (the CRH paradox), but near term this switches to contraction-promotion. CRH does NOT continuously inhibit contractions throughout pregnancy — that is the role of progesterone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','easy','mcq','Which hormone primarily drives milk synthesis (lactogenesis) after delivery?','["A. Oxytocin","B. Oestrogen","C. Prolactin","D. Progesterone"]','C','Prolactin, secreted by lactotroph cells of the anterior pituitary, is the primary hormone driving milk synthesis (lactogenesis). During pregnancy, high oestrogen and progesterone block prolactin action at the breast. After delivery, the fall in placental oestrogen/progesterone allows prolactin to initiate milk production within 2–3 days.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','easy','mcq','The milk ejection (let-down) reflex is mediated by which hormone released from the posterior pituitary?','["A. Prolactin","B. ADH (vasopressin)","C. FSH","D. Oxytocin"]','D','Oxytocin released from the posterior pituitary (triggered by infant suckling via the neuroendocrine milk ejection reflex) causes contraction of myoepithelial cells surrounding mammary alveoli and ducts, ejecting milk. Oxytocin also helps uterine involution postpartum. The reflex can also be conditioned (infant crying triggers let-down).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','mcq','Colostrum produced in the first few days postpartum differs from mature milk in that colostrum has:','["A. Higher protein, IgA, leukocytes, and growth factors; lower fat and lactose","B. Higher fat and lactose, lower protein and IgA","C. Higher caloric content per mL than mature milk","D. Lower sodium and higher potassium than mature milk"]','A','Colostrum (days 1–5) is a thick yellow fluid with higher protein (~87 g/L vs 9 g/L in mature milk), very high secretory IgA (passive immunity), leukocytes, growth factors, and lactoferrin, but lower fat and lactose than mature milk. It is uniquely suited to the newborn gut and immune system.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','mcq','Breastfeeding suppresses ovulation primarily by which mechanism?','["A. Prolactin directly inhibiting LH release from the anterior pituitary","B. Suckling-induced prolactin rise suppressing pulsatile GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus","C. Oxytocin from the posterior pituitary blocking follicular development","D. Prolactin stimulating progesterone to suppress FSH"]','B','Frequent suckling stimulates prolactin secretion and simultaneously inhibits hypothalamic GnRH pulsatility (via dopamine and opioid mechanisms). Reduced GnRH → reduced FSH and LH → anovulation. The contraceptive effect (Lactational Amenorrhoea Method) requires exclusive breastfeeding, infant age <6 months, and maternal amenorrhoea — 98% effective when all three criteria are met.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','hard','mcq','Prolactin levels are high during pregnancy yet lactation does not occur. What inhibits prolactin action on the breast during pregnancy?','["A. Inhibin from the placenta blocks prolactin receptors","B. Dopamine from placenta directly suppresses prolactin secretion","C. High oestrogen and progesterone block prolactin action at the mammary gland receptor/post-receptor level","D. hCG competes with prolactin at mammary lactotroph receptors"]','C','During pregnancy, prolactin levels rise progressively but lactation does not occur because high circulating oestrogen and progesterone from the placenta block prolactin''s action at the mammary gland (at receptor or post-receptor signalling level). After placental delivery, abrupt fall in these hormones allows prolactin to initiate lactogenesis II within 30–72 hours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','mcq','Prolactin secretion is under tonic inhibitory control. Which hypothalamic factor primarily maintains this inhibition?','["A. GnRH","B. Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH)","C. Somatostatin","D. Dopamine (prolactin-inhibiting factor)"]','D','Dopamine released from tuberoinfundibular dopaminergic (TIDA) neurons is the primary prolactin-inhibiting factor (PIF), acting on D2 receptors on lactotrophs. Dopamine antagonists (metoclopramide, domperidone, antipsychotics) cause hyperprolactinaemia. TRH is a prolactin-releasing factor (explains elevated prolactin in hypothyroidism).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','easy','mcq','Uterine involution (return to non-pregnant size) is typically complete by:','["A. 6 weeks postpartum","B. 2 weeks postpartum","C. 3 months postpartum","D. 6 months postpartum"]','A','Uterine involution is complete by approximately 6 weeks postpartum. The uterus decreases from ~1 kg at delivery to ~60–80 g via autolysis of extra muscle cells, decrease in individual myometrial cell size, and ischaemic necrosis. Breastfeeding accelerates involution via oxytocin release.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','hard','mcq','Sheehan''s syndrome following massive postpartum haemorrhage is caused by:','["A. Autoimmune destruction of anterior pituitary lactotrophs by postpartum antibodies","B. Ischaemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary due to haemorrhage-induced hypotension","C. DIC causing thrombosis of the posterior pituitary","D. Excessive oxytocin causing pituitary cell apoptosis"]','B','Sheehan''s syndrome results from ischaemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary (which hypertrophies during pregnancy, increasing metabolic demand) secondary to severe hypotension following massive postpartum haemorrhage. First sign: failure of lactation (prolactin deficiency). Later: amenorrhoea, hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency. The posterior pituitary is usually spared (different blood supply via inferior hypophyseal artery).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','medium','mcq','A breastfeeding mother at 4 weeks postpartum has fever, painful erythematous breast, and malaise. The most likely causative organism is:','["A. Streptococcus pyogenes","B. Bacteroides fragilis","C. Staphylococcus aureus","D. Escherichia coli"]','C','Lactational mastitis affects ~10% of breastfeeding women, most commonly in the first 3 months. Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA) enters through nipple fissures or abrasions and causes localised cellulitis with fever, malaise, and painful erythematous breast. Treatment: continue breastfeeding/pumping, dicloxacillin or flucloxacillin. If fluctuant, suspect abscess requiring drainage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Lactation & Postpartum','hard','mcq','Hindmilk (end of feed) differs from foremilk (start of feed) in that hindmilk has:','["A. Higher volume and lower fat content","B. Lower fat but higher immunoglobulin content","C. Higher lactose and lower protein content","D. Higher fat content (3–4× more) providing the majority of caloric intake"]','D','Foremilk has lower fat content (~1–2%) but higher volume. Hindmilk has much higher fat content (5–8%), providing ~50% of total caloric intake. Incomplete feeds (frequent short feeds) leave the infant getting predominantly foremilk, potentially causing lactose overload and poor weight gain. Feeding to completion from one breast is recommended.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','easy','mcq','Which cells in the testis produce testosterone in response to LH stimulation?','["A. Leydig (interstitial) cells","B. Sertoli cells","C. Spermatogonia","D. Peritubular myoid cells"]','A','Leydig (interstitial) cells are located in the connective tissue between seminiferous tubules and produce testosterone in response to LH. Sertoli cells (within seminiferous tubules) support spermatogenesis, produce inhibin B, androgen-binding protein (ABP), and respond to FSH.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','easy','mcq','Spermatogenesis takes approximately how long from spermatogonium to mature spermatozoon?','["A. 14 days","B. 64–74 days","C. 35 days","D. 90–120 days"]','B','Spermatogenesis takes approximately 64–74 days in humans (about 2 months). An additional 12 days is required for epididymal maturation where sperm acquire motility and fertilising capacity. This is clinically relevant: scrotal hyperthermia effects on sperm quality manifest 2–3 months later.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','mcq','The blood-testis barrier is formed by tight junctions between which cells, and what is its primary function?','["A. Leydig cells — prevents LH from reaching spermatocytes","B. Peritubular myoid cells — maintains luminal fluid pressure","C. Sertoli cells — creates immunologically privileged environment for post-meiotic germ cells","D. Spermatogonia — prevents apoptotic signals from reaching spermatids"]','C','Sertoli cells form the blood-testis barrier via tight junctions between adjacent cells, dividing the seminiferous tubule into a basal compartment (spermatogonia) and adluminal compartment (spermatocytes onward). The BTB prevents immune recognition of post-meiotic germ cells (which express novel antigens) and creates a specialised hormonal environment for spermatogenesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','mcq','Dihydrotestosterone (DHT), produced by 5α-reductase from testosterone, is responsible for which effects?','["A. Development of internal male genitalia (vas deferens, epididymis)","B. Pubertal growth spurt and muscle mass increase","C. Spermatogenesis within the seminiferous tubules","D. External genitalia development (penis, scrotum) and prostate; male pattern baldness; benign prostatic hypertrophy"]','D','Testosterone mediates internal genitalia development, spermatogenesis, pubertal muscle/bone effects, libido, and the growth spurt. DHT (produced by 5α-reductase type 2 in genital skin and prostate) mediates external genitalia masculinisation in fetal life, prostate growth, benign prostatic hyperplasia, and male pattern baldness. 5α-reductase deficiency causes 46,XY DSD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','mcq','Inhibin B produced by Sertoli cells selectively suppresses which pituitary hormone?','["A. FSH","B. LH","C. GH","D. TSH"]','A','Inhibin B produced by Sertoli cells selectively suppresses FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary without affecting LH. This allows independent regulation of FSH (spermatogenesis) and LH (testosterone production). Low inhibin B (impaired Sertoli function) causes elevated FSH — a clinical marker of spermatogenic failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','hard','mcq','A 30-year-old male with azoospermia has elevated FSH and LH, low testosterone, and small firm testes. Karyotype is 47,XXY. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (Kallmann syndrome)","B. Klinefelter syndrome — extra X chromosome causes progressive seminiferous tubule hyalinisation","C. Obstructive azoospermia with epididymal obstruction","D. Y chromosome AZFc microdeletion"]','B','Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) is the most common chromosomal cause of male infertility. The extra X chromosome causes progressive seminiferous tubule hyalinisation and fibrosis (elevated FSH), Leydig cell dysfunction (low testosterone, elevated LH), small firm testes, and azoospermia — a hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism pattern.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','easy','mcq','Sperm undergo final maturation and acquire motility and fertilising capacity in which structure?','["A. Seminiferous tubules","B. Rete testis","C. Epididymis","D. Vas deferens"]','C','Sperm produced in the seminiferous tubules pass through the rete testis and efferent ductules into the epididymis, where they undergo maturation over ~12 days and acquire progressive motility and the capacity to fertilise an oocyte. The epididymis also concentrates and stores sperm before ejaculation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','hard','mcq','The rate-limiting step in testosterone synthesis in Leydig cells is:','["A. Conversion of testosterone to DHT by 5α-reductase","B. Aromatisation of testosterone to oestradiol","C. Conversion of androstenedione to testosterone by 17β-HSD","D. Transport of cholesterol from the outer to inner mitochondrial membrane by StAR protein"]','D','The rate-limiting step in steroid hormone synthesis is the transport of cholesterol from the outer to inner mitochondrial membrane by the Steroidogenic Acute Regulatory (StAR) protein. LH → cAMP → PKA → StAR phosphorylation → accelerated cholesterol transport → pregnenolone synthesis → testosterone. Mutations in StAR cause congenital lipoid adrenal hyperplasia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','medium','mcq','Which accessory sex gland contributes approximately 60% of seminal fluid volume and provides fructose as the primary energy substrate for sperm?','["A. Seminal vesicles","B. Bulbourethral (Cowper''s) glands","C. Prostate gland","D. Epididymis"]','A','The seminal vesicles contribute ~60% of ejaculate volume and secrete fructose (primary sperm energy substrate), prostaglandins, and semenogelin. The prostate gland contributes ~30% (PSA, zinc, citric acid). The bulbourethral glands secrete pre-ejaculatory alkaline mucus that neutralises urethral acidity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Male Reproductive Physiology','hard','mcq','A 35-year-old man has asthenospermia, recurrent respiratory tract infections since childhood, and situs inversus on chest CT. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Cystic fibrosis — CFTR mutation causing bilateral absence of the vas deferens","B. Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome) — dynein arm defect causing impaired cilia and sperm flagella motility","C. Y chromosome AZFb microdeletion causing maturation arrest","D. Varicocele causing heat-induced axoneme damage"]','B','Kartagener syndrome (a subset of primary ciliary dyskinesia) is caused by dynein arm protein mutations (DNAI1, DNAH5), resulting in defective ciliary/flagellar motility. Features: situs inversus (50% — random organ determination without normal ciliary flow), bronchiectasis, sinusitis, and male infertility (immotile spermatozoa with 9+2 axonemal structure but absent functional dynein arms).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','easy','mcq','The first sign of puberty in girls is:','["A. Pubic hair growth (pubarche)","B. Menarche","C. Breast bud development (thelarche)","D. Growth spurt"]','C','In girls, puberty typically begins with thelarche (breast bud development) at approximately 8–13 years. The sequence is: thelarche → pubarche (pubic hair) → growth spurt → menarche (approximately 2 years after thelarche). In boys, testicular enlargement (>4 mL / 2.5 cm) is the first sign.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','easy','mcq','The onset of puberty is triggered by increased pulsatile secretion of which hypothalamic hormone?','["A. Prolactin","B. CRH","C. TRH","D. GnRH"]','D','Puberty is initiated by increased frequency and amplitude of pulsatile GnRH secretion from hypothalamic GnRH neurons. This reawakening of the GnRH pulse generator drives increased FSH and LH secretion, leading to gonadal sex steroid production. Kisspeptin (Kiss1) neurons in the arcuate nucleus are key upstream regulators.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','mcq','Loss-of-function mutations in the kisspeptin receptor KISS1R (GPR54) cause:','["A. Hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism — failure to undergo puberty","B. Precocious puberty","C. Premature ovarian insufficiency","D. Polycystic ovarian syndrome"]','A','Kisspeptin neurons are the key upstream regulators of GnRH pulsatility. KISS1R is expressed on GnRH neurons. Loss-of-function mutations cause absent GnRH pulses → hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism with failure of puberty (similar to Kallmann syndrome but with intact olfaction). Activating mutations cause central precocious puberty.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','mcq','Adrenarche (increased adrenal DHEA/DHEA-S secretion) normally causes which primary clinical effects?','["A. Testicular/ovarian enlargement","B. Pubic and axillary hair growth; adult body odour; mild acne","C. The pubertal growth spurt","D. Breast development in girls"]','B','Adrenarche (zona reticularis maturation, beginning ~6–8 years) causes pubarche (pubic and axillary hair growth), adult body odour (apocrine gland activation), and mild acne — due to peripheral conversion of adrenal DHEA-S to testosterone. Adrenarche is independent of the HPG axis; it does not cause genital or breast development.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','mcq','The average age of natural menopause is approximately:','["A. 40–45 years","B. 45–48 years","C. 51–52 years","D. 55–57 years"]','C','The average age of natural menopause is 51–52 years, ranging from 45–55 years. Premature ovarian insufficiency (POI) is menopause before age 40. Smokers experience menopause 1–2 years earlier. The perimenopausal transition typically begins 2–8 years before the final period.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','easy','mcq','Following menopause, which hormonal pattern is expected?','["A. Low FSH, low LH, low oestradiol","B. High oestradiol with suppressed FSH","C. Normal FSH and LH with low progesterone only","D. High FSH, high LH, low oestradiol"]','D','After menopause, ovaries no longer respond to FSH/LH (follicular depletion with reduced inhibin and oestrogen). Without negative feedback, FSH rises markedly (>40 IU/L) and LH rises. Oestradiol falls to <30 pmol/L. Oestrone (from peripheral aromatisation of adrenal androstenedione in adipose tissue) becomes the predominant oestrogen postmenopausally.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','hard','mcq','A 14-year-old girl has no pubertal signs. Investigations show FSH 45 IU/L, LH 30 IU/L, oestradiol <20 pmol/L, karyotype 45,XO. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Turner syndrome — ovarian dysgenesis causing hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism","B. Constitutional delay of puberty","C. Kallmann syndrome — GnRH neuron migration failure","D. Hypothyroidism suppressing GnRH pulsatility"]','A','Turner syndrome (45,XO or mosaic) causes streak gonads (fibrous tissue replacing ovaries) that fail to produce oestrogen or inhibin. Without negative feedback, FSH and LH are markedly elevated (hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism). Features: short stature, webbed neck, widely spaced nipples, coarctation of the aorta, primary amenorrhoea. Treatment: oestrogen replacement to induce puberty.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','hard','mcq','The hot flush (vasomotor symptom) of menopause is best explained by:','["A. Elevated FSH acting directly on cutaneous blood vessels","B. Oestrogen deficiency narrowing the thermoregulatory neutral zone making core temperature unstable","C. Progesterone deficiency causing hypothalamic hypersensitivity","D. Increased noradrenaline causing episodic vasodilation"]','B','Oestrogen deficiency narrows the thermoregulatory neutral zone in the hypothalamus. Small rises in core temperature that would normally be tolerated trigger heat dissipation responses (vasodilation, sweating). Oestrogen therapy widens the neutral zone, abolishing flushes. Kisspeptin/neurokinin B neurons in the hypothalamus are also implicated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','medium','mcq','A 10-year-old boy has Tanner stage 4 pubic hair, testicular volume 15 mL, and markedly elevated testosterone with suppressed FSH and LH. The most likely diagnosis is:','["A. Central (gonadotrophin-dependent) precocious puberty","B. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia with adrenal androgen excess","C. Peripheral (gonadotrophin-independent) precocious puberty — e.g. activating LH receptor mutation","D. Hypothyroidism causing gonadotrophin elevation"]','C','Suppressed FSH and LH with elevated testosterone indicates gonadotrophin-independent (peripheral) precocious puberty (testotoxicosis). Causes: activating LH receptor mutations (familial male-limited precocious puberty), McCune–Albright syndrome. Central precocious puberty has elevated FSH and LH. CAH causes elevated adrenal androgens (not testosterone) with suppressed gonadotrophins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Anatomy & Physiology','Puberty & Menopause','hard','mcq','The earliest detectable hormonal change of perimenopause is:','["A. Fall in oestradiol","B. Fall in progesterone in the luteal phase","C. Rise in LH preceding FSH","D. Rise in early follicular phase FSH due to reduced inhibin B from diminishing follicle pool"]','D','The earliest hormonal change of perimenopause is a rise in early follicular phase FSH (day 3 FSH >10–12 IU/L), reflecting declining inhibin B secretion as the follicle pool diminishes. Oestradiol may paradoxically be normal or even elevated early in perimenopause as elevated FSH drives remaining follicles harder. The FSH rise and inhibin B fall occur years before oestrogen levels fall significantly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
